{"name":"Aurora Splendour","hex":"#595682","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aurora","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An atmospheric phenomenon created by charged particles from the sun striking the upper atmosphere, creating coloured lights in the sky. It is usually named australis or borealis based on whether it is in the Southern or Northern Hemisphere respectively.","The Roman goddess of the dawn, equivalent to the Greek Ἠώς (Ēṓs)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aurora"},{"term":"splendour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["British, Canadian, Ireland, South Africa, Australian, and New Zealand standard spelling of splendor."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/splendour"}],"entries":[{"title":"Splendour (play)","blocks":[{"text":"Splendour is a play by Welsh playwright and screenwriter Abi Morgan. It was first performed at the Edinburgh festival at the Traverse Theatre in 2000 in a production by Paines Plough."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Splendour_(play)","wikidata":"Q55633718"},{"title":"Splendour (apple)","blocks":[{"text":"Splendour, Splendor or Starksplendor is a modern cultivar of domesticated apple which was developed in New Zealand, and is regarded there as a popular commercial dessert apple. It has been said to be a cross between 'Red Dougherty' and 'Golden Delicious', but genetic analysis has not definitely characterized either of the parent cultivars, and records do not indicate known or suspected parents."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Cross_section_of_Splendour%2C_National_Fruit_Collection_%28acc._1961-074%29.jpg/330px-Cross_section_of_Splendour%2C_National_Fruit_Collection_%28acc._1961-074%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Splendour_(apple)","wikidata":"Q19840849"},{"title":"Aurora","blocks":[{"text":"An aurora is a natural light display in Earth's upper atmosphere caused by charged particles from the Sun colliding with atoms in the atmosphere. These collisions excite oxygen and nitrogen, which then emit light of different colours such as green, red, and purple. When observed in high-latitude regions they are called polar lights and aurora polaris. In the Arctic they are called the northern lights or aurora borealis; in the Antarctic, the term southern lights or aurora australis is used. Auroras display dynamic patterns of radiant light that appear as curtains, rays, spirals or dynamic flickers covering the entire sky."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Aurora_borealis_over_Eielson_Air_Force_Base%2C_Alaska.jpg/330px-Aurora_borealis_over_Eielson_Air_Force_Base%2C_Alaska.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora","wikidata":"Q40609"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Splendour_on_tree%2C_National_Fruit_Collection_%28acc._1961-074%29.jpg/500px-Splendour_on_tree%2C_National_Fruit_Collection_%28acc._1961-074%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Splendour_on_tree%2C_National_Fruit_Collection_%28acc._1961-074%29.jpg/500px-Splendour_on_tree%2C_National_Fruit_Collection_%28acc._1961-074%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Splendours are late-ripening","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASplendour_on_tree%2C_National_Fruit_Collection_(acc._1961-074).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Flames_in_the_sky.jpg/500px-Flames_in_the_sky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Flames_in_the_sky.jpg/1280px-Flames_in_the_sky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&ut